<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tenshō embassy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1582–1590</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tenshō embassy was a mission of four young Japanese Christian nobles sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in the name of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> lords <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomo-sorin">Ōtomo Sōrin</a>, Ōmura Sumitada and Arima Harunobu. Organised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> Alessandro Valignano, it left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> in 1582, was received by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-xiii">Pope Gregory XIII</a>, and returned in 1590 with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press. It was the first Japanese embassy to Europe.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tensho-embassy.jpg" alt="Tenshō embassy" width="320" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: German 'newspaper' Newe Zeyttung · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_Japanese_Embassy_to_Europe_1586.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
